1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,174
Sweetheart, the most beautiful Christmas house I

2
00:00:03,174 --> 00:00:05,687
ever walked into had almost nothing in

3
00:00:05,687 --> 00:00:05,886
it.

4
00:00:06,066 --> 00:00:08,270
White walls, a little tree,

5
00:00:08,270 --> 00:00:10,230
candles in every window,

6
00:00:10,230 --> 00:00:13,496
and straw stars hanging from red thread.

7
00:00:13,676 --> 00:00:15,545
It belonged to Mrs.

8
00:00:15,725 --> 00:00:18,863
Lindqvist, a Swedish widow at our church

9
00:00:18,863 --> 00:00:21,844
in Mansfield, in nineteen fifty-eight.

10
00:00:22,024 --> 00:00:23,133
I was fourteen,

11
00:00:23,133 --> 00:00:25,424
and I stood in her doorway with

12
00:00:25,424 --> 00:00:26,459
my mouth open.

13
00:00:26,808 --> 00:00:29,113
Today people pay a fortune for that

14
00:00:29,113 --> 00:00:31,615
Nordic Christmas look in the catalogs.

15
00:00:31,795 --> 00:00:35,038
So tonight I'm giving you nine Scandinavian

16
00:00:35,038 --> 00:00:38,809
Christmas decorations that look expensive but cost

17
00:00:38,809 --> 00:00:40,317
about a dollar each,

18
00:00:40,317 --> 00:00:41,222
the way Mrs.

19
00:00:41,402 --> 00:00:43,097
Lindqvist made them.

20
00:00:43,277 --> 00:00:44,535
And stay with me,

21
00:00:44,535 --> 00:00:47,125
because the last one is the Swedish

22
00:00:47,125 --> 00:00:49,789
secret that makes a whole house feel

23
00:00:49,789 --> 00:00:52,304
warm, and it's not a decoration at

24
00:00:52,304 --> 00:00:52,600
all.

25
00:00:52,950 --> 00:00:54,019
Mrs.

26
00:00:54,199 --> 00:00:56,755
Lindqvist came from a little town in

27
00:00:56,755 --> 00:00:59,737
Sweden, where winter nights start at three

28
00:00:59,737 --> 00:01:00,944
in the afternoon.

29
00:01:01,124 --> 00:01:03,628
She said when it's dark that long,

30
00:01:03,628 --> 00:01:06,133
you learn to make light and warmth

31
00:01:06,133 --> 00:01:07,753
out of almost nothing.

32
00:01:07,933 --> 00:01:09,909
Straw from the barn,

33
00:01:09,909 --> 00:01:12,082
wood scraps, red yarn,

34
00:01:12,082 --> 00:01:13,959
paper, and candles.

35
00:01:14,139 --> 00:01:15,428
Nothing was bought.

36
00:01:15,608 --> 00:01:17,595
Everything was made by hand,

37
00:01:17,595 --> 00:01:19,086
at the kitchen table,

38
00:01:19,086 --> 00:01:20,577
in the long evenings.

39
00:01:20,926 --> 00:01:23,307
The rule in her house was simple.

40
00:01:23,487 --> 00:01:26,149
White, red, natural wood,

41
00:01:26,149 --> 00:01:27,213
and green.

42
00:01:27,393 --> 00:01:28,485
That's all.

43
00:01:28,665 --> 00:01:31,791
No tinsel, no blinking lights,

44
00:01:31,791 --> 00:01:32,937
no plastic.

45
00:01:33,117 --> 00:01:35,332
She said too many colors make a

46
00:01:35,332 --> 00:01:38,260
room loud, and Christmas should be quiet.

47
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:40,772
My mother came home from her house

48
00:01:40,772 --> 00:01:43,310
and took half the decorations off our

49
00:01:43,310 --> 00:01:43,653
tree.

50
00:01:43,833 --> 00:01:46,376
My father didn't notice for a week.

51
00:01:46,725 --> 00:01:48,511
So here's the color rule,

52
00:01:48,511 --> 00:01:51,083
honey, and it's the first thing that

53
00:01:51,083 --> 00:01:52,798
makes it look expensive.

54
00:01:52,978 --> 00:01:54,666
Pick white and red,

55
00:01:54,666 --> 00:01:57,598
with natural wood and real green.

56
00:01:57,778 --> 00:02:00,843
Put away everything else for a year.

57
00:02:01,023 --> 00:02:02,946
In a minute I'll show you the

58
00:02:02,946 --> 00:02:05,399
first decoration, and you can make it

59
00:02:05,399 --> 00:02:07,853
tonight from a bag of drinking straws

60
00:02:07,853 --> 00:02:09,510
or a few stalks of wheat.

61
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:10,836
Number one.

62
00:02:11,016 --> 00:02:12,409
Straw stars.

63
00:02:12,589 --> 00:02:15,454
In Sweden they use real wheat straw,

64
00:02:15,454 --> 00:02:18,080
soaked in warm water so it bends.

65
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:20,241
You can buy a bundle at a

66
00:02:20,241 --> 00:02:22,458
craft store for two dollars,

67
00:02:22,458 --> 00:02:24,042
or use paper straws.

68
00:02:24,222 --> 00:02:26,200
Lay four straws in a cross,

69
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:28,398
like a star with eight points,

70
00:02:28,398 --> 00:02:30,669
and tie them in the middle with

71
00:02:30,669 --> 00:02:33,452
red thread, weaving it over and under.

72
00:02:33,632 --> 00:02:35,455
Snip the ends to a point.

73
00:02:35,804 --> 00:02:37,278
Hang them on the tree,

74
00:02:37,278 --> 00:02:38,282
in the windows,

75
00:02:38,282 --> 00:02:40,425
or from a branch over the table.

76
00:02:40,605 --> 00:02:43,423
They catch the candlelight and glow like

77
00:02:43,423 --> 00:02:43,775
gold.

78
00:02:43,955 --> 00:02:44,953
Mrs.

79
00:02:45,133 --> 00:02:47,864
Lindqvist made fifty every December,

80
00:02:47,864 --> 00:02:50,215
and she gave one to every child

81
00:02:50,215 --> 00:02:50,973
at church.

82
00:02:51,153 --> 00:02:54,323
I still have mine from nineteen fifty-eight.

83
00:02:54,503 --> 00:02:56,669
It's a little crooked now,

84
00:02:56,669 --> 00:02:58,253
honey, but so am I.

85
00:02:58,602 --> 00:02:59,972
Number two.

86
00:03:00,152 --> 00:03:02,451
The Julbock, the Yule goat.

87
00:03:02,631 --> 00:03:05,844
It's an old Scandinavian Christmas figure,

88
00:03:05,844 --> 00:03:08,292
a little goat made of straw tied

89
00:03:08,292 --> 00:03:09,516
with red ribbon.

90
00:03:09,696 --> 00:03:12,648
Long ago, people said the goat brought

91
00:03:12,648 --> 00:03:15,211
the presents, before Santa Claus.

92
00:03:15,391 --> 00:03:17,385
You can buy them small,

93
00:03:17,385 --> 00:03:19,814
or make one from a bundle of

94
00:03:19,814 --> 00:03:22,676
straw, bent and tied into a body,

95
00:03:22,676 --> 00:03:25,538
legs, a head and two curly horns.

96
00:03:25,887 --> 00:03:28,212
Put a little one under the tree,

97
00:03:28,212 --> 00:03:30,029
or a big one by the front

98
00:03:30,029 --> 00:03:30,392
door.

99
00:03:30,572 --> 00:03:34,069
My grandchildren thought it was the funniest

100
00:03:34,069 --> 00:03:35,658
thing they ever saw,

101
00:03:35,658 --> 00:03:37,248
a goat at Christmas.

102
00:03:37,428 --> 00:03:39,371
Now they won't let me put the

103
00:03:39,371 --> 00:03:40,644
tree up without it.

104
00:03:40,824 --> 00:03:42,749
One year it went missing,

105
00:03:42,749 --> 00:03:44,904
and we found it in the dog's

106
00:03:44,904 --> 00:03:45,212
bed.

107
00:03:45,392 --> 00:03:47,577
The dog had decided it was his

108
00:03:47,577 --> 00:03:48,887
Christmas present.

109
00:03:49,236 --> 00:03:50,467
Number three.

110
00:03:50,647 --> 00:03:52,458
Woven paper hearts.

111
00:03:52,638 --> 00:03:54,391
These come from Denmark.

112
00:03:54,571 --> 00:03:56,682
Fold two strips of paper,

113
00:03:56,682 --> 00:03:58,539
one red and one white,

114
00:03:58,539 --> 00:04:00,987
in half, round the top like a

115
00:04:00,987 --> 00:04:03,603
heart, and weave them together,

116
00:04:03,603 --> 00:04:04,869
over and under.

117
00:04:05,049 --> 00:04:07,659
It makes a little checkered basket shaped

118
00:04:07,659 --> 00:04:08,486
like a heart.

119
00:04:08,666 --> 00:04:10,547
Children love making them.

120
00:04:10,727 --> 00:04:13,038
You can fill them with candy and

121
00:04:13,038 --> 00:04:14,628
hang them on the tree.

122
00:04:14,977 --> 00:04:16,589
If this is helping you,

123
00:04:16,589 --> 00:04:18,971
honey, tap the like button for me.

124
00:04:19,151 --> 00:04:22,019
It helps Grandma more than you know.

125
00:04:22,199 --> 00:04:24,730
And here's a tip for the hearts.

126
00:04:24,910 --> 00:04:26,130
Use heavy paper,

127
00:04:26,130 --> 00:04:29,637
like old greeting cards or construction paper,

128
00:04:29,637 --> 00:04:31,467
not thin wrapping paper,

129
00:04:31,467 --> 00:04:33,374
so they hold their shape.

130
00:04:33,554 --> 00:04:35,768
And make a little handle from a

131
00:04:35,768 --> 00:04:36,839
strip of paper.

132
00:04:37,019 --> 00:04:38,076
Mrs.

133
00:04:38,256 --> 00:04:41,429
Lindqvist filled hers with raisins and nuts,

134
00:04:41,429 --> 00:04:43,809
and the children ate them off the

135
00:04:43,809 --> 00:04:45,396
tree on Christmas Eve.

136
00:04:45,745 --> 00:04:47,034
Number four.

137
00:04:47,214 --> 00:04:48,526
Felt ornaments.

138
00:04:48,706 --> 00:04:51,202
Little red and white felt hearts,

139
00:04:51,202 --> 00:04:53,093
stars, birds and animals,

140
00:04:53,093 --> 00:04:56,346
stitched around the edges with white thread

141
00:04:56,346 --> 00:04:58,842
and stuffed with a little cotton.

142
00:04:59,022 --> 00:05:01,430
A sheet of felt costs about fifty

143
00:05:01,430 --> 00:05:03,619
cents and makes six ornaments.

144
00:05:03,799 --> 00:05:06,005
You don't need to sew well.

145
00:05:06,185 --> 00:05:09,491
Big, simple stitches look homemade and charming,

146
00:05:09,491 --> 00:05:11,212
which is the whole point.

147
00:05:11,562 --> 00:05:13,651
And make felt animals,

148
00:05:13,651 --> 00:05:14,221
honey.

149
00:05:14,401 --> 00:05:16,177
A little reindeer,

150
00:05:16,177 --> 00:05:17,658
a mouse, a fox,

151
00:05:17,658 --> 00:05:18,349
a bird.

152
00:05:18,529 --> 00:05:19,799
Cut two shapes,

153
00:05:19,799 --> 00:05:21,579
stitch them together,

154
00:05:21,579 --> 00:05:22,511
stuff them.

155
00:05:22,691 --> 00:05:25,153
Give them a tiny black bead for

156
00:05:25,153 --> 00:05:25,709
an eye.

157
00:05:25,889 --> 00:05:28,798
My granddaughter made a whole family of

158
00:05:28,798 --> 00:05:30,364
felt mice one winter,

159
00:05:30,364 --> 00:05:31,706
with tiny scarves.

160
00:05:31,886 --> 00:05:34,365
They live on my bookshelf all year

161
00:05:34,365 --> 00:05:36,844
round now, because I can't bear to

162
00:05:36,844 --> 00:05:37,865
put them away.

163
00:05:38,215 --> 00:05:40,303
So far, the color rule,

164
00:05:40,303 --> 00:05:42,755
straw stars, the Yule goat,

165
00:05:42,755 --> 00:05:45,750
paper hearts, and felt ornaments.

166
00:05:45,930 --> 00:05:48,040
Number five is the one that makes

167
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:50,087
your house glow from the street.

168
00:05:50,267 --> 00:05:52,275
A candle in every window.

169
00:05:52,455 --> 00:05:55,084
In Sweden, they put a little wooden

170
00:05:55,084 --> 00:05:57,112
candle arch in the windows,

171
00:05:57,112 --> 00:05:58,539
or a single candle.

172
00:05:58,719 --> 00:06:01,608
Use battery candles with a warm light,

173
00:06:01,608 --> 00:06:02,444
on a timer,

174
00:06:02,444 --> 00:06:04,268
so they come on at dusk.

175
00:06:04,618 --> 00:06:05,988
And Mrs.

176
00:06:06,168 --> 00:06:09,596
Lindqvist told me about Saint Lucia's Day,

177
00:06:09,596 --> 00:06:11,799
the thirteenth of December.

178
00:06:11,979 --> 00:06:14,828
Before dawn, the oldest daughter in the

179
00:06:14,828 --> 00:06:17,166
house puts on a white dress with

180
00:06:17,166 --> 00:06:18,993
a red sash and a crown of

181
00:06:18,993 --> 00:06:20,381
greens and candles,

182
00:06:20,381 --> 00:06:23,084
and brings coffee and saffron buns to

183
00:06:23,084 --> 00:06:24,472
her parents in bed.

184
00:06:24,652 --> 00:06:27,084
Her daughter did it every year in

185
00:06:27,084 --> 00:06:27,821
Mansfield.

186
00:06:28,001 --> 00:06:30,038
I asked my mother if I could

187
00:06:30,038 --> 00:06:30,474
do it.

188
00:06:30,654 --> 00:06:33,493
She said, not with real candles on

189
00:06:33,493 --> 00:06:34,996
your head, Ruthie.

190
00:06:35,346 --> 00:06:36,658
Number six.

191
00:06:36,838 --> 00:06:38,324
Natural wood.

192
00:06:38,504 --> 00:06:41,082
A few slices of birch log as

193
00:06:41,082 --> 00:06:42,463
a candle stand.

194
00:06:42,643 --> 00:06:45,801
A plain wooden bowl filled with pinecones.

195
00:06:45,981 --> 00:06:48,322
A bare branch from the yard,

196
00:06:48,322 --> 00:06:49,827
standing in a jar,

197
00:06:49,827 --> 00:06:53,087
hung with straw stars and paper hearts.

198
00:06:53,267 --> 00:06:56,898
That's a Scandinavian Christmas tree for almost

199
00:06:56,898 --> 00:06:57,516
nothing.

200
00:06:57,696 --> 00:06:58,729
Mrs.

201
00:06:58,909 --> 00:07:01,601
Lindqvist had one in her kitchen every

202
00:07:01,601 --> 00:07:04,294
year, and the children were allowed to

203
00:07:04,294 --> 00:07:05,144
decorate it.

204
00:07:05,493 --> 00:07:07,552
And bring in the real green,

205
00:07:07,552 --> 00:07:08,361
sweetheart.

206
00:07:08,541 --> 00:07:10,894
A few cut evergreen branches in a

207
00:07:10,894 --> 00:07:13,603
pitcher of water on the kitchen table.

208
00:07:13,783 --> 00:07:16,514
A sprig of juniper over a picture

209
00:07:16,514 --> 00:07:17,011
frame.

210
00:07:17,191 --> 00:07:20,073
A small potted spruce or rosemary tree

211
00:07:20,073 --> 00:07:20,983
by the door,

212
00:07:20,983 --> 00:07:23,030
with one straw star on top.

213
00:07:23,210 --> 00:07:25,626
Real green is what makes a Nordic

214
00:07:25,626 --> 00:07:28,041
room feel alive instead of empty,

215
00:07:28,041 --> 00:07:30,457
and you can get the branches free

216
00:07:30,457 --> 00:07:31,628
at the tree lot.

217
00:07:31,977 --> 00:07:33,185
Number seven.

218
00:07:33,365 --> 00:07:34,758
Clove oranges.

219
00:07:34,938 --> 00:07:36,033
Take an orange,

220
00:07:36,033 --> 00:07:38,662
and press whole cloves into the skin

221
00:07:38,662 --> 00:07:39,977
in rows or swirls.

222
00:07:40,157 --> 00:07:42,715
Tie a red ribbon around it and

223
00:07:42,715 --> 00:07:44,932
hang it, or pile them in a

224
00:07:44,932 --> 00:07:45,358
bowl.

225
00:07:45,538 --> 00:07:47,999
They smell like Christmas for weeks.

226
00:07:48,179 --> 00:07:51,174
They dry out slowly and last until

227
00:07:51,174 --> 00:07:51,790
spring.

228
00:07:51,970 --> 00:07:53,798
A bag of cloves and a few

229
00:07:53,798 --> 00:07:56,210
oranges cost about three dollars,

230
00:07:56,210 --> 00:07:58,623
and the smell is worth a hundred.

231
00:07:58,972 --> 00:08:01,624
Use a toothpick to poke the holes

232
00:08:01,624 --> 00:08:04,276
first, honey, so the cloves go in

233
00:08:04,276 --> 00:08:07,169
easy and your thumbs don't get sore.

234
00:08:07,349 --> 00:08:09,446
Danny and I made them every year

235
00:08:09,446 --> 00:08:10,821
at the kitchen table,

236
00:08:10,821 --> 00:08:13,049
and the whole house smelled like a

237
00:08:13,049 --> 00:08:13,769
spice shop.

238
00:08:13,949 --> 00:08:16,367
My mother hung them in the closets,

239
00:08:16,367 --> 00:08:18,370
too, to keep the moths out of

240
00:08:18,370 --> 00:08:19,406
the wool coats.

241
00:08:19,756 --> 00:08:20,894
Number eight.

242
00:08:21,074 --> 00:08:22,258
A white table.

243
00:08:22,438 --> 00:08:25,298
A white tablecloth or a plain white

244
00:08:25,298 --> 00:08:26,932
runner, red napkins,

245
00:08:26,932 --> 00:08:29,873
a few straw stars scattered down the

246
00:08:29,873 --> 00:08:33,630
middle, candles, and some sprigs of evergreen.

247
00:08:33,810 --> 00:08:35,018
That's all.

248
00:08:35,198 --> 00:08:37,887
A white table looks clean and elegant,

249
00:08:37,887 --> 00:08:40,222
and the food looks even better on

250
00:08:40,222 --> 00:08:40,434
it.

251
00:08:40,614 --> 00:08:41,601
Mrs.

252
00:08:41,781 --> 00:08:44,976
Lindqvist served cinnamon buns on a white

253
00:08:44,976 --> 00:08:48,015
table, and they looked like a painting.

254
00:08:48,365 --> 00:08:51,526
And put the red and white everywhere

255
00:08:51,526 --> 00:08:52,843
in small doses,

256
00:08:52,843 --> 00:08:53,369
honey.

257
00:08:53,549 --> 00:08:55,942
A red and white checked dish towel

258
00:08:55,942 --> 00:08:56,997
in the kitchen.

259
00:08:57,177 --> 00:08:59,589
Red mittens hung on a string over

260
00:08:59,589 --> 00:09:00,393
the window.

261
00:09:00,573 --> 00:09:02,773
A white wool blanket with a red

262
00:09:02,773 --> 00:09:05,043
stripe over the back of a chair.

263
00:09:05,223 --> 00:09:07,685
A little red wooden horse on the

264
00:09:07,685 --> 00:09:09,763
windowsill, the Dala horse.

265
00:09:09,943 --> 00:09:12,961
It's the repetition of those two colors,

266
00:09:12,961 --> 00:09:15,074
quietly, all over the house,

267
00:09:15,074 --> 00:09:17,640
that makes it look like a catalog.

268
00:09:17,990 --> 00:09:19,504
And now number nine,

269
00:09:19,504 --> 00:09:22,077
the Swedish secret I promised you.

270
00:09:22,257 --> 00:09:24,486
It's not a decoration at all.

271
00:09:24,666 --> 00:09:26,101
It's called mys,

272
00:09:26,101 --> 00:09:27,894
or hygge in Denmark.

273
00:09:28,074 --> 00:09:30,001
It means coziness.

274
00:09:30,181 --> 00:09:32,538
A warm blanket on the sofa.

275
00:09:32,718 --> 00:09:34,567
Candles lit at supper,

276
00:09:34,567 --> 00:09:36,584
every night of December,

277
00:09:36,584 --> 00:09:38,349
not just on holidays.

278
00:09:38,529 --> 00:09:41,374
Hot cocoa or spiced cider on the

279
00:09:41,374 --> 00:09:41,907
stove.

280
00:09:42,087 --> 00:09:44,807
Time sitting together in the soft light,

281
00:09:44,807 --> 00:09:46,371
without the television.

282
00:09:46,721 --> 00:09:47,673
Mrs.

283
00:09:47,853 --> 00:09:51,312
Lindqvist said, the decorations are only to

284
00:09:51,312 --> 00:09:52,921
make the room ready,

285
00:09:52,921 --> 00:09:53,484
Ruthie.

286
00:09:53,664 --> 00:09:55,859
The Christmas is the people in it.

287
00:09:56,039 --> 00:09:58,672
I've thought about that every December for

288
00:09:58,672 --> 00:09:59,800
sixty-eight years.

289
00:09:59,980 --> 00:10:02,436
You can have every straw star in

290
00:10:02,436 --> 00:10:05,583
Sweden, and if nobody sits down together,

291
00:10:05,583 --> 00:10:06,888
it's just a room.

292
00:10:07,068 --> 00:10:08,371
Light the candles,

293
00:10:08,371 --> 00:10:10,760
and call the family to the table.

294
00:10:11,110 --> 00:10:12,747
So one more time.

295
00:10:12,927 --> 00:10:15,492
White, red, wood and green,

296
00:10:15,492 --> 00:10:17,107
and nothing else.

297
00:10:17,287 --> 00:10:18,483
Straw stars.

298
00:10:18,663 --> 00:10:20,265
A little Yule goat.

299
00:10:20,445 --> 00:10:22,256
Woven paper hearts.

300
00:10:22,436 --> 00:10:23,748
Felt ornaments.

301
00:10:23,928 --> 00:10:25,913
A candle in every window.

302
00:10:26,093 --> 00:10:28,903
Natural wood and a branch tree.

303
00:10:29,083 --> 00:10:30,662
Clove oranges.

304
00:10:30,842 --> 00:10:32,084
A white table.

305
00:10:32,264 --> 00:10:33,908
And the cozy part,

306
00:10:33,908 --> 00:10:35,004
every night.

307
00:10:35,184 --> 00:10:36,717
That's how Mrs.

308
00:10:36,897 --> 00:10:40,059
Lindqvist made the most beautiful house I

309
00:10:40,059 --> 00:10:42,295
ever saw, for almost nothing.

310
00:10:42,645 --> 00:10:44,540
Tell me in the comments,

311
00:10:44,540 --> 00:10:45,409
sweetheart.

312
00:10:45,589 --> 00:10:48,023
Does your family have a tradition from

313
00:10:48,023 --> 00:10:49,048
the old country?

314
00:10:49,228 --> 00:10:52,022
Swedish, German, Polish,

315
00:10:52,022 --> 00:10:53,884
Irish, anything.

316
00:10:54,064 --> 00:10:55,666
I'd love to hear it.

317
00:10:55,846 --> 00:10:59,143
The whole playlist of Grandma's Christmas tips

318
00:10:59,143 --> 00:11:01,293
is right there on your screen,

319
00:11:01,293 --> 00:11:03,299
and I'll see you in the next

320
00:11:03,299 --> 00:11:03,729
video.

321
00:11:03,909 --> 00:11:05,570
Good night, honey.

322
00:11:05,750 --> 00:11:07,944
God jul, as Mrs.

323
00:11:08,124 --> 00:11:10,098
Lindqvist used to say.
